# Erdos Problem #99 - flat-center degree-four activation dichotomy

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** C-II, canonical contact-minimal full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** low-t flat-center amplification; canonical unloaded-degree lower bound; loaded contact-degree cap; triangle-free unit-contact geometry  
**Claim class:** proof-only structural refinement; theorem-state authority unchanged

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The low-t flat-center theorem removes the old multiplicity-five loss: on the
small-residual branch there are linearly many **distinct** source vertices `m` that are
the distinguished middle of a pentagonal boundary chain

\[
l-m-r,
\qquad |l-m|=|m-r|=1,
\qquad \alpha_m:=\angle lmr=\pi-o(1).
\]

The same local geometry forces substantially more structure at every such center.
For all sufficiently large sources:

1. every flat center has contact degree at most four;
2. an unloaded flat center therefore has degree three or four, while a loaded flat
   center has degree at most three by the canonical loaded-degree cap;
3. every degree-four flat center produces two endpoint-disjoint hidden near-contacts
   between its contact neighbors, and these hidden gaps have total excess `o(1)` per
   center.

Consequently, if `V_flat` is the flat-center population and `V_4` is the number of
flat centers of contact degree four, there is a family `H_4` of pairwise
endpoint-disjoint hidden source pairs such that

\[
\boxed{|H_4|\ge {2V_4\over9}.}
\tag{FD.1}
\]

Every pair `xy in H_4` has a common flat center `m` with

\[
|m-x|=|m-y|=1,
\qquad
1<|x-y|=1+o(1),
\tag{FD.2}
\]

and on the diagonal flatness scale the whole selected family obeys

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{xy\in H_4}(|x-y|-1)=o(D).
}
\tag{FD.3}
\]

Thus the low-t small-residual branch has the following source-level alternative:

\[
\boxed{
V_{\le3}\ge {V_{flat}\over2}
\quad\text{or}\quad
|H_4|\ge {V_{flat}\over9},
}
\tag{FD.4}
\]

where `V_{<=3}` counts flat centers of contact degree at most three.
In the first branch, splitting by loaded status gives the sharper nested alternative

\[
\boxed{
V_{loaded}\ge {V_{flat}\over4}
\quad\text{or}\quad
V_{u,3}\ge {V_{flat}\over4},
}
\tag{FD.5}
\]

unless the degree-four hidden-contact branch of (FD.4) already holds. Here `V_{u,3}`
counts unloaded degree-three flat centers.

Near the current proof-only low-t floor, where the flat-center theorem gives

\[
{V_{flat}\over D}\ge0.4944166723690961\ldots-o(1),
\]

(FD.4)--(FD.5) imply at least one of

\[
\boxed{
{V_{loaded}\over D}\ge0.1236041680922740\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{FD.6}
\]

\[
\boxed{
{V_{u,3}\over D}\ge0.1236041680922740\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{FD.7}
\]

or

\[
\boxed{
{|H_4|\over D}\ge0.0549351858187884\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{FD.8}
\]

with the `L^1` hidden-gap conclusion (FD.3).

Throughout the broader conditional band `t<=1.8585`, the corresponding safe
coefficients are `0.1000970668294269...` in (FD.6)--(FD.7) and
`0.0444875852575231...` in (FD.8).

---

## 1. Angular separation of contact rays

Fix a source vertex `m`. If `x,y` are two distinct unit-contact neighbors, then source
unit separation gives

\[
|x-y|\ge1.
\]

If `beta in (0,pi]` is the smaller angle between the rays `mx,my`, then

\[
|x-y|=2\sin(\beta/2),
\]

so

\[
\boxed{\beta\ge\pi/3.}
\tag{FD.9}
\]

In the triangle-free branch equality is impossible: `|x-y|=1` would make `mxy` an
exact unit equilateral triangle. Thus every cyclic gap between consecutive contact rays
is strictly larger than `pi/3`.

---

## 2. A sufficiently flat middle has contact degree at most four

Choose the diagonal flatness scale from the low-t flat-center theorem so that every
retained center satisfies

\[
\alpha_m>\pi-\epsilon_D,
\qquad \epsilon_D\to0.
\tag{FD.10}
\]

The pentagon sector at `m` is one cyclic gap between consecutive contact rays. Suppose
`d_C(m)>=5`. Besides that gap there are at least four other cyclic contact-ray gaps,
each strictly larger than `pi/3` by (FD.9). Therefore

\[
2\pi
>
(\pi-\epsilon_D)+4{\pi\over3}.
\]

For all sufficiently large `D`, say `epsilon_D<pi/3`, the right side exceeds `2pi`, a
contradiction. Hence

\[
\boxed{d_C(m)\le4.}
\tag{FD.11}
\]

Canonical C-II structure says every unloaded positive-contact vertex has contact
degree at least three, while every loaded vertex has contact degree at most three.
Thus an unloaded flat center has degree `3` or `4`, and every degree-four flat center
is automatically unloaded.

---

## 3. A degree-four flat center creates three complementary near-contacts

Let `m` have degree four. List its four contact neighbors cyclically so that the large
pentagon sector is the gap from `l` to `r`, of size

\[
\alpha_m=\pi-\varepsilon_m,
\qquad 0<\varepsilon_m\le\epsilon_D.
\tag{FD.12}
\]

Write the other two contact neighbors as `u,v` in the complementary order

\[
r,u,v,l.
\]

Let the three complementary angular gaps be `beta_1,beta_2,beta_3`. Their sum is

\[
\beta_1+\beta_2+\beta_3
=2\pi-\alpha_m
=\pi+\varepsilon_m.
\tag{FD.13}
\]

Each `beta_j>pi/3`, so putting

\[
e_j:=\beta_j-\pi/3>0
\]

gives the exact identity

\[
\boxed{e_1+e_2+e_3=\varepsilon_m.}
\tag{FD.14}
\]

The three consecutive neighbor pairs

\[
(r,u),\qquad(u,v),\qquad(v,l)
\]

are source pairs with a common unit-contact neighbor `m`. Their distances are

\[
d_j=2\sin(\beta_j/2)>1.
\tag{FD.15}
\]

Because the derivative of `2 sin(beta/2)` is `cos(beta/2)<=1`,

\[
0<d_j-1\le e_j.
\tag{FD.16}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{j=1}^3(d_j-1)\le\varepsilon_m\le\epsilon_D.
}
\tag{FD.17}
\]

In particular all three hidden neighbor-neighbor distances are `1+o(1)`. This is a
local consequence of the flat **middle** and does not use the separate leaf-tooth
hidden diagonals of the pentagon.

---

## 4. Two endpoint-disjoint hidden pairs per degree-four center

From every degree-four flat center keep the first and third complementary pairs

\[
(r,u),\qquad(v,l).
\tag{FD.18}
\]

They are endpoint-disjoint within that center. Across all degree-four centers this
produces `2V_4` hidden-pair objects.

A source vertex `x` can be an endpoint of such an object only when it is a unit-contact
neighbor of the corresponding center `m`. The triangle-free unit-contact degree bound
is at most five, so `x` can be adjacent to at most five selected centers. Since it is
used in at most one of the two chosen pairs for each center, `x` occurs in at most five
hidden-pair objects.

Therefore the conflict graph on the `2V_4` hidden pairs, joining pairs that share an
endpoint, has maximum degree at most

\[
(5-1)+(5-1)=8.
\tag{FD.19}
\]

A greedy independent set retains at least one ninth of the objects, proving (FD.1).

The low-t flat-center population is `O(D)` on every convergent normalized subsequence.
Summing (FD.17) over all degree-four centers therefore gives at most

\[
V_4\epsilon_D=o(D)
\]

total hidden-side excess before extraction. Passing to the endpoint-disjoint subfamily
can only decrease this nonnegative sum, proving (FD.3).

---

## 5. Global degree/activation split

The distinct flat centers partition as

\[
V_{flat}=V_{\le3}+V_4.
\tag{FD.20}
\]

If `V_4>=V_flat/2`, equation (FD.1) gives

\[
|H_4|\ge{2\over9}{V_{flat}\over2}={V_{flat}\over9}.
\]

Otherwise `V_{<=3}>=V_flat/2`. Every unloaded vertex in this class has degree exactly
three. Splitting `V_{<=3}` into loaded and unloaded vertices, one of the two classes
has size at least `V_flat/4`. This proves (FD.4)--(FD.5).

At the current low-t floor use

\[
V_{flat}/D\ge0.4944166723690961\ldots-o(1).
\]

Division by four and nine gives (FD.6)--(FD.8). The `t<=1.8585` constants follow in
the same way from the existing flat-center floor

\[
V_{flat}/D\ge0.4003882673177079\ldots-o(1).
\]

---

## 6. Why this is a new bridge

The low-t small-residual survivor now has a finite three-way source-level obstruction:

1. linearly many **loaded** almost-straight centers, directly exposing a large subset
   of the Woodall diameter graph vertices;
2. linearly many **unloaded degree-three** almost-straight centers, each carrying one
   additional exact contact ray in the complementary half-plane; or
3. linearly many endpoint-disjoint hidden near-contacts created by degree-four centers,
   with only `o(D)` total gap to activation.

The third branch is structurally different from the pentagon leaf-tooth extraction:
its hidden pairs lie between neighbors of the flat middle and are forced solely by the
contact-degree-four geometry. The first two branches identify exactly which KKT vertex
type must carry the flat-center sea if this extra activation family is absent.

No exact hidden contact, simultaneous activation motion, or strict sub-`D` competitor
is claimed here. The next target is to combine branch (1) with near-extremal Woodall
caterpillar/odd-cycle topology, or branch (2) with the single extra contact ray and the
outer-train phase system; branch (3) is ready for an `L^1` simultaneous-contact
activation argument.

## 7. Scope audit

- Flat centers are actual source vertices and are distinct by the preceding low-t
  flat-center theorem.
- The degree-four hidden pairs are actual source pairs, not train-graph abstractions.
- Strict `d_j>1` uses triangle-freeness; no inactive pair is silently promoted to a
  contact.
- The matching denominator `9` uses only the unit-contact degree cap five and two
  endpoint-disjoint pairs per center.
- (FD.3) uses the diagonal scale `epsilon_D->0` and the already-known `O(D)` flat-center
  count; it does not require a quantitative residual-to-angle modulus.
- Contact-isolated source points remain in the source.
- C-II and Level I remain open.
